meta {
  name: Create Quote (Node A)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: http://{{adminA}}/quotes
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "walletAddress": "test.com/sender",
    "paymentIntentUrl": "{{receiverIntentUrl}}",
    "debitAmount": "100"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  const body = res.getBody();
  if (body?.id) {
    bru.setVar("quoteId", body.id);
  }
  if (body?.paymentIntentId) {
    bru.setVar("quotePaymentIntentId", body.paymentIntentId);
  }
}

docs {
  Creates the quote on **Node A** against Node B's remote intent.

  Uses `paymentIntentUrl` (not `paymentIntentId`). Because that URL points at
  `open-payments-resource-service-b` - which does not start with Node A's own
  `OPEN_PAYMENTS_URL` (`http://open-payments-resource-service:3002`) - Node A
  classifies it as **remote**: it fetches Node B's incoming payment server-side,
  generates a **new local payment-intent UUID**, resolves the peer by the
  destination ILP address, and requests an ILP quote from the peer.

  Save both returned ids: `quoteId` and `paymentIntentId` (`quotePaymentIntentId`
  - this new local UUID, not step 1's id, is what step 3 uses).

  ## Expected failure today
  The ILP quoting probe hits the connector's loopback transport, which rejects
  with `F99 "loopback: no upstream peer connected"`, so the quote never
  transitions pending -> completed and this call errors/times out. This is the
  point the flow reaches until an HTTP ILP transport + `/ilp` ingress exist.
}
